 1 big thing: Anthropic IPO spotlights AI risks 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images Anthropic's IPO filing , out today, includes an extensive list of extreme warnings — including the chance its own technology could put humanity at risk, Axios' Madison Mills reports. 

 These are the wildest IPO risk factors in history — a jarring collision of Wall Street legalese and what some see as Silicon Valley sci-fi. ⚠️ In over 90 pages of risk factors, Anthropic warns that ...

 AI could pose "catastrophic or existential risks to humanity," and could try to manipulate humans. AI models are becoming harder to monitor, and their abilities could exceed human reviewers' ability to do so. AI models have exhibited behavior resembling blackmail, including attempts to resist being shut down. 👾 Anthropic also disclosed that four of its models gained unauthorized access to real third-party systems during cybersecurity evaluations.

 In one incident, a model used a discovered password to gain administrator access, collected additional credentials, changed system settings and accessed an individual's personal information. Anthropic said it found no evidence that the models pursued goals beyond their assigned tasks or attempted to evade oversight. 🖼️ The big picture: Every company has to lay out risks for investors to consider when they go public, but Anthropic is the first in modern history to go to such existential extremes.

 Go deeper ... More on Anthropic's IPO . 
 
